Recognize taps and flings with Flutter's constants and rules, and drive the recognizer by the executor's clock.

What: adds `gestures/constants.rs` (public as `gpui::constants`), a port of Flutter's constants.dart @ 3.50.1 with
Flutter's names (`kTouchSlop`, `kDoubleTapTimeout`, ...), the one copy GPUI's recognizers and Parallax's platform
behaviour table read. `GestureTuning::default()` holds those constants (the same on every platform): `touch_slop`
kTouchSlop 18 px (was 8), `multi_tap_interval` kDoubleTapTimeout 300 ms (was 400), `multi_tap_slop` kDoubleTapSlop 100
px (was 16), the new `multi_tap_min_interval` kDoubleTapMinTime 40 ms, `long_press_duration` kLongPressTimeout 500 ms,
and the fling limits of its `ScrollPhysics::ios()` (`BouncingScrollPhysics`: `min_fling_velocity` kMinFlingVelocity * 2
= 100 px/s, was 50), so the defaults are one Flutter platform, not a mix; the fling ceiling is kMaxFlingVelocity (was
a private copy of it). `ScrollPhysics` gains Flutter's `min_fling_distance` (kTouchSlop), `min_fling_velocity` and
`max_fling_velocity`, and the public `Slops` / `slops(kind, touch_slop)` port events.dart's `computeHitSlop`,
`computePanSlop` and `computeScaleSlop`, the one copy of that rule. A tap's click count follows
`DoubleTapGestureRecognizer`: it continues the sequence when the new touch starts less than the interval after the
previous tap's up, within the slop of where the previous tap started, at least the min interval after the previous
tap started, and on the same target (the innermost hitbox under the touch: the window passes it to the recognizer);
otherwise it starts a new sequence at 1. GPUI kept counting past 2 (triple click) and still does. A release flings
only as Flutter's `isFlingGesture` allows: velocity above the minimum and the velocity estimate's offset along the
axis (0011's `VelocityEstimate.offset`) beyond the physics' `min_fling_distance`. `TouchGestureRecognizer::handle_event`, `pending_long_press` and `tick_momentum` take `now`
from the window's `cx.background_executor().now()` instead of `Instant::now()`. `Window::set_gesture_tuning`
(`#[cfg(test)]`) lets a test pass its own tuning.

Why: Flutter's recognizer tests, ported as table rows (`gestures/recognizer_tests.rs`, below), fail on GPUI's tuning
and tap counting: 5 of 44 rows. An 8 px slop makes a 17 px wiggle a pan ("Short distance does not cancel tap") and
starts a pan at 14 px ("Should recognize pan"); with no minimum interval, a touch screen's split touch counts as a
double tap ("Should not recognize two over-rapid taps", "Over-rapid taps resets double tap…", "No duplicate tap
events"). GPUI counts taps window-wide, so with Flutter's 100 px double-tap slop a tap on one button and then on
another 60 px away counted as a double tap on the second; Flutter's recognizer only sees its own widget's pointers.
A slow drag (60 px/s over 6 px) flung, where Flutter's minimum fling distance stops it. Flutter raised its slop from 8 to 18 after complaints that targets were too hard to hit. The wall clock made
the recognizer impossible to drive from GPUI's test platform (the executor docs: "Calling this instead of
`std::time::Instant::now` allows the use of fake timers in tests"); the long-press timer already ran on the executor,
so its deadline and its timer now read the same clock. 0003's pointer-source test relied on wall time passing between
dispatches for its fling; it now advances the test clock 8 ms per input.

Test: `gestures::recognizer_tests::flutter_recognizer_tests_pass`, 44 rows from tap_test.dart (12),
double_tap_test.dart (10), long_press_test.dart (11) and drag_test.dart (11), and two per-target rows (same target
counts 2, another target 1). Each row is a recorded touch trace
(time, touch id, phase, position) replayed into a test window, Flutter's tuning passed explicitly, and the gestures
the window dispatched with their times (tap with click count, scroll steps, claimed long press and touch drag). Arena,
button, device-kind, multi-pointer and velocity cases are not rows: docs/gesture-recognizers.md lists them.
`fling_needs_flutters_velocity_and_distance` checks the fling rule (a 6 px slow drag doesn't fling, 30 px does).
The trace player is `gpui::play_trace` (test.rs, `test-support`), public so recorded traces from any crate replay
the same way: `(at_ms, PlatformInput)` pairs (touches, wheels, trackpads) dispatched on the test executor's clock,
an optional frame period that runs the window's next-frame callbacks (fling momentum), and a callback after each
input and frame (`TraceStep`).

Ported code: constants.dart (`gestures/constants.rs`, whole), the slop functions (events.dart), the tap-count rule
(multitap.dart), the fling rule (monodrag.dart), the fling limits (scroll_physics.dart), and the test rows (the four
test files). Copyright 2014 The Flutter Authors, BSD-3-Clause.

Size: ~1500 lines, ~890 of them the new test table and ~120 constants.rs; the recognizer and window changes are ~450,
most of it rustfmt rewrapping test call sites that gained the `now` and target arguments (Flutter ports are exempt
from the size limit).

Upstream PR: not filed yet.


